{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,110p' pose-mcp/internal/pose/material_binding.go; sed -n '650,735p;1090,1185p' pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate.go; cat pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_publish.go; sed -n '180,225p' pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_scope.go; cat .pose/rules/knowledge-governance.md; rg --files .pose/knowledge | rg 'carry|validat'\"","aggregated_output":"package pose\n\nimport (\n\t\"crypto/sha256\"\n\t\"encoding/hex\"\n\t\"encoding/json\"\n\t\"path\"\n\t\"path/filepath\"\n\t\"sort\"\n\t\"strings\"\n)\n\n// Validation evidence binds to the material it validated (spec\n// pose-validation-evidence-material-binding, ADR\n// validation-evidence-binds-to-validated-material). A commit whose every\n// changed path is a derived administrative record does not change what the\n// checks exercised, so it does not move the material digest.\n\n// engineVersion is the running engine, registered by the binaries (internal/pose\n// stays importable on its own). It is part of every material digest, so a new\n// engine never inherits evidence the previous one produced.\nvar engineVersion = \"unregistered\"\n\n// SetEngineVersion registers the running engine for material digests.\nfunc SetEngineVersion(v string) { engineVersion = v }\n\n// administrativeDirs is the closed list of derived-record directories. It is\n// owned by the engine; adding a class is a contract change that needs an ADR.\nvar administrativeDirs = []string{\".pose/indexes/\", \".pose/results/\", \".pose/state/\", \".pose/assessments/\"}\n\n// materialIndexes are files inside an administrative directory that still\n// change what the checks demand.\nvar materialIndexes = map[string]bool{\".pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json\": true}\n\n// IsAdministrativePath reports whether p is a derived administrative record.\nfunc IsAdministrativePath(p string) bool {\n\tif p == \"\" || p != path.Clean(p) || strings.HasPrefix(p, \"/\") || strings.HasPrefix(p, \"../\") {\n\t\treturn false\n\t}\n\tif materialIndexes[p] {\n\t\treturn false\n\t}\n\tif !recordExtensions[strings.ToLower(path.Ext(p))] {\n\t\treturn false\n\t}\n\tfor _, dir := range administrativeDirs {\n\t\tif strings.HasPrefix(p, dir) {\n\t\t\treturn true\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tif rest, ok := strings.CutPrefix(p, \".pose/\"); ok {\n\t\tif i := strings.Index(rest, \"/\"); i > 0 && strings.HasPrefix(rest[:i], \"review-\") {\n\t\t\treturn true\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn false\n}\n\n// recordExtensions are the data formats a derived record is written in. Code or\n// a script stored in a class is material: that keeps a source file moved or\n// copied into a class from hiding its later edits. A copied data file stays\n// indistinguishable from a record; a check that reads data out of a derived-record\n// directory is outside this contract (ADR validation-evidence-binds-to-validated-material).\nvar recordExtensions = map[string]bool{\".json\": true, \".jsonl\": true, \".ndjson\": true, \".md\": true, \".txt\": true, \".log\": true, \".transcript\": true}\n\n// TreeEntry is one entry of the validated head's tree.\ntype TreeEntry struct{ Mode, Blob string }\n\nfunc regularMode(mode string) bool { return mode == \"100644\" || mode == \"100755\" }\n\n// MaterialInputs are what a material digest is computed from: the paths a change\n// set names (both sides of a rename, and the validation matrix) and the\n// destinations of renames. They are stored with the set so the digest can be\n// recomputed against the current tree without the commits' own records.\ntype MaterialInputs struct {\n\tNames []string `json:\"names\"`\n\tMoved []string `json:\"moved,omitempty\"`\n}\n\n// MaterialInputsOf derives the inputs from per-commit path records. They must\n// be unmerged: folding a create and a later rename into one creation would hide\n// that the file moved into a class.\nfunc MaterialInputsOf(paths []ObservedPath) MaterialInputs {\n\tnames := map[string]bool{\".pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json\": true}\n\tmoved := map[string]bool{}\n\tfor _, p := range paths {\n\t\tfor _, name := range []string{p.Path, p.OldPath, p.NewPath} {\n\t\t\tif name != \"\" {\n\t\t\t\tnames[name] = true\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t\t// A rename destination is material whatever directory it lands in:\n\t\t// otherwise moving a source file into a class would hide its later edits.\n\t\tif p.OldPath != \"\" && p.NewPath != \"\" {\n\t\t\tmoved[p.NewPath] = true\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tin := MaterialInputs{}\n\tfor name := range names {\n\t\tin.Names = append(in.Names, name)\n\t}\n\tfor name := range moved {\n\t\tin.Moved = append(in.Moved, name)\n\t}\n\tsort.Strings(in.Names)\n\tsort.Strings(in.Moved)\n\treturn in\n}\n\n// MaterialSetDigest digests the head state of the material a change set\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn checks, nil\n}\n\ntype validationModule struct {\n\tRel, Abs, Stack string\n}\n\nfunc confinedRelativePath(path string) bool {\n\tif strings.TrimSpace(path) == \"\" {\n\t\treturn true\n\t}\n\tclean := filepath.Clean(filepath.FromSlash(path))\n\treturn !filepath.IsAbs(clean) && clean != \"..\" && !strings.HasPrefix(clean, \"..\"+string(filepath.Separator))\n}\n\nfunc discoverValidationModulesIn(files posemodel.Files, root string) ([]validationModule, error) {\n\t// testdata/fixture(s): see index.go's scanModules for why these are\n\t// excluded (spec pose-upgrade-path-audit-fixes).\n\tignored := map[string]bool{\".git\": true, \".qwen\": true, \"node_modules\": true, \"vendor\": true, \".venv\": true, \".pnpm-store\": true, \"target\": true, \"dist\": true, \"build\": true, \".next\": true, \"coverage\": true, \".pose\": true, \"testdata\": true, \"fixture\": true, \"fixtures\": true}\n\t// A gitignored subtree (a locally-checked-out project the repository\n\t// deliberately excludes, e.g. a vendored tool) is never a governed\n\t// deliverable module — see posemodel.GitIgnoredPaths (spec\n\t// pose-discovery-gitignore-and-root-alias-fix).\n\tgitIgnored := posemodel.GitIgnoredPathsIn(files, root)\n\tbyPath := map[string]string{}\n\terr := posemodel.WalkDirIn(files, root, func(path string, entry os.DirEntry, err error) error {\n\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn err\n\t\t}\n\t\tif entry.IsDir() {\n\t\t\tif path != root && ignored[entry.Name()] {\n\t\t\t\treturn filepath.SkipDir\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif path != root {\n\t\t\t\tif rel, relErr := filepath.Rel(root, path); relErr == nil && (gitIgnored[filepath.ToSlash(rel)+\"/\"] || gitIgnored[filepath.ToSlash(rel)]) {\n\t\t\t\t\treturn filepath.SkipDir\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\treturn nil\n\t\t}\n\t\t// spec pose-stack-catalog-expansion: the Python manager (poetry/\n\t\t// pipenv/pip/setuptools/pep517) is resolved by the matrix `when`\n\t\t// predicates, not at discovery time — every marker maps to the\n\t\t// \"python\" stack. stackForManifestFile is shared with scanModules\n\t\t// (spec pose-validation-scanner-consolidation) so a stack-detection\n\t\t// fix here applies there too.\n\t\tstack := stackForManifestFile(entry.Name())\n\t\tif stack != \"\" {\n\t\t\t// Existing overwrite-by-walk-order semantics preserved\n\t\t\t// unchanged for compatibility; `pose stacks` surfaces the full\n\t\t\t// per-directory catalog resolution (including any conflicting\n\t\t\t// markers) independently of what this single-stack map picks.\n\t\t\tbyPath[filepath.Dir(path)] = stack\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn nil\n\t})\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn nil, err\n\t}\n\tmodules := make([]validationModule, 0, len(byPath))\n\tfor abs, stack := range byPath {\n\t\tif stack == \"java\" && isAndroidModuleIn(files, abs) {\n\t\t\t// A Gradle module carrying an AndroidManifest.xml is an Android\n\t\t\t// app/library, not a generic JVM backend module — conflating the\n\t\t\t// two under \"java\" made every Android module's domain\n\t\t\t// misleading (spec pose-upgrade-path-audit-fixes). Kotlin-only\n\t\t\t// non-Android Gradle modules are unaffected: they have no\n\t\t\t// AndroidManifest.xml and keep classifying as \"java\".\n\t\t\tstack = \"android\"\n\t\t}\n\t\trel, err := filepath.Rel(root, abs)\n\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn nil, err\n\t\t}\n\t\tmodules = append(modules, validationModule{filepath.ToSlash(rel), abs, stack})\n\t}\n\tsort.Slice(modules, func(i, j int) bool { return modules[i].Rel < modules[j].Rel })\n\treturn modules, nil\n}\n\n// discoverValidationModules reads and writes the filesystem directly; discoverValidationModulesIn takes the\n// operation's files.\nfunc discoverValidationModules(root string) ([]validationModule, error) {\n\treturn discoverValidationModulesIn(posemodel.NativeFiles(), root)\n\t\t}\n\t\tmoduleFilter = resolved\n\t}\n\tknownModules := map[string]bool{}\n\tfor _, module := range modules {\n\t\tknownModules[module.Rel] = true\n\t}\n\tfor rel, override := range matrix.ModuleOverrides {\n\t\tclean := filepath.ToSlash(filepath.Clean(rel))\n\t\tif clean == \"..\" || strings.HasPrefix(clean, \"../\") || filepath.IsAbs(rel) {\n\t\t\tfmt.Fprintf(stderr, cliText(locale, \"Error: moduleOverrides contains a path outside the project: %s\\n\", \"Erro: moduleOverrides contém path fora do projeto: %s\\n\"), rel)\n\t\t\treturn 2\n\t\t}\n\t\tif knownModules[clean] {\n\t\t\tif override.Stack != \"\" {\n\t\t\t\tfor idx := range modules {\n\t\t\t\t\tif modules[idx].Rel == clean {\n\t\t\t\t\t\tmodules[idx].Stack = override.Stack\n\t\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tabs := filepath.Join(root, filepath.FromSlash(clean))\n\t\tif info, err := os.Stat(abs); err == nil && info.IsDir() {\n\t\t\tstack := override.Stack\n\t\t\tif stack == \"\" {\n\t\t\t\tstack = \"contract\"\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tmodules = append(modules, validationModule{Rel: clean, Abs: abs, Stack: stack})\n\t\t\tknownModules[clean] = true\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tsort.Slice(modules, func(i, j int) bool { return modules[i].Rel < modules[j].Rel })\n\tvar scope *scopeSelection\n\tif changedTo != \"\" && changedFrom == \"\" {\n\t\tfmt.Fprintln(stderr, cliText(locale, \"Error: --changed-to requires --changed-from.\", \"Erro: --changed-to exige --changed-from.\"))\n\t\treturn 2\n\t}\n\tif changedFrom != \"\" {\n\t\tsel, err := computeChangedScope(root, changedFrom, changedTo, modules)\n\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\tfmt.Fprintf(stderr, cliText(locale, \"Error: changed-scope selection: %v\\n\", \"Erro: seleção por escopo alterado: %v\\n\"), err)\n\t\t\treturn 2\n\t\t}\n\t\tscope = &sel\n\t\tif explain {\n\t\t\tfmt.Fprintf(stdout, \"[changed-scope] %s: %d changed file(s), %d/%d module(s) selected\\n\", sel.rangeLabel(), len(sel.Changed), len(sel.Selected), len(modules))\n\t\t\tfor _, m := range modules {\n\t\t\t\tif reason, ok := sel.Selected[m.Rel]; ok {\n\t\t\t\t\tfmt.Fprintf(stdout, \"  + %s: %s\\n\", m.Rel, reason)\n\t\t\t\t} else {\n\t\t\t\t\tfmt.Fprintf(stdout, \"  - %s: not affected by %s\\n\", m.Rel, sel.rangeLabel())\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\trun := validationRunResult{\n\t\tSchemaVersion: validationResultSchema,\n\t\tGeneratedAt:   time.Now().UTC().Format(time.RFC3339),\n\t\tMode:          mode,\n\t\tStackFilter:   stackFilter,\n\t\tModuleFilter:  moduleFilter,\n\t\tChecks:        []checkResult{},\n\t}\n\tmatrixDigest := sha256.Sum256(raw)\n\trun.MatrixSHA256 = \"sha256:\" + hex.EncodeToString(matrixDigest[:])\n\trun.EngineVersion = validationEngineIdentity()\n\t// The head is pinned before anything is planned: selection, carried\n\t// results and the end-of-run comparison all name this one commit (review\n\t// round 5, N14).\n\trun.GitHead = pinnedHead\n\tvar carryState carryPlan\n\tif carry {\n\t\tcarryState = planCarry(root, canonicalResultsPath, mode, run.MatrixSHA256, run.GitHead, matrix, modules)\n\t\trun.Carry = carryState.record\n\t\tout := render(stdout, stderr)\n\t\tif !carryState.record.Used {\n\t\t\tout.ContractLine(\"[carry] full run: \" + carryState.record.Reason)\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tout.ContractLine(fmt.Sprintf(\"[carry] base %s: %d changed file(s), %d/%d module(s) run\", carryState.record.BaseHead, carryState.record.Changed, len(carryState.record.Selected), len(modules)))\n\t\t\tif explain {\n\t\t\t\tfor _, m := range modules {\n\t\t\t\t\tif reason, ok := carryState.record.Selected[m.Rel]; ok {\n\t\t\t\t\t\tout.ContractLine(\"  + \" + m.Rel + \": \" + reason)\n\t\t\t\t\t} else {\n\t\t\t\t\t\tout.ContractLine(\"  = \" + m.Rel + \": carried from \" + carryState.record.BaseHead)\n\t\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tif graph, err := buildCurrentDeliveryGraph(root); err == nil {\n\t\trun.ProvenanceDigest = graph.ProvenanceDigest\n\t\trun.ScopeProvenance = map[string]string{}\n\t\tseenSpecs := map[string]bool{}\ncat: pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_publish.go: No such file or directory\n\t\t\tfor _, dep := range declaredDependencyHits(file, meta.Modules[m.Rel].DependsOn) {\n\t\t\t\tif dep != owner {\n\t\t\t\t\tselectModule(m.Rel, \"declared dependency \"+dep+\" changed: \"+file)\n\t\t\t\t\tbreak\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif owner == \"\" && (m.Rel == \".\" || !meta.Modules[m.Rel].SelfContained) {\n\t\t\t\tselectModule(m.Rel, \"root-level change outside any module: \"+file)\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\t// Policy widening: high-criticality modules always run.\n\tfor _, m := range modules {\n\t\tif meta.Modules[m.Rel].Criticality == \"high\" {\n\t\t\tif _, ok := sel.Selected[m.Rel]; !ok {\n\t\t\t\tsel.Selected[m.Rel] = \"policy: criticality high always runs\"\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\t// Transitive dependents of selected modules (declared edges only).\n\tfor changed := true; changed; {\n\t\tchanged = false\n\t\tfor _, m := range modules {\n\t\t\tif _, ok := sel.Selected[m.Rel]; ok {\n\t\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tfor _, dep := range meta.Modules[m.Rel].DependsOn {\n\t\t\t\tif reason, ok := sel.Selected[filepath.ToSlash(filepath.Clean(dep))]; ok {\n\t\t\t\t\tsel.Selected[m.Rel] = \"depends on selected module \" + dep + \" (\" + firstScopeReason(reason) + \")\"\n\t\t\t\t\tchanged = true\n\t\t\t\t\tbreak\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn sel, nil\n}\n\n// firstScopeReason keeps chained dependency reasons short and readable.\nfunc firstScopeReason(reason string) string {\n\tif i := strings.Index(reason, \" (\"); i > 0 {\n\t\treturn reason[:i]\n\t}\n\treturn reason\n}\n\n# Rule: Knowledge Governance\n\n## When to consult\n\nConsult this guide when creating, updating, reviewing, or removing artifacts under `.pose/knowledge/`.\n\n## TTL and retention\n\n- Set `expires_at` on every artifact when it is created.\n- Use a default TTL of 30 days for notes, decision logs, and handoffs.\n- Use a TTL of up to 90 days only with a justification recorded in the artifact body.\n- Treat an artifact without `expires_at` as non-compliant and block its creation or merge.\n\n## When this is not the right home\n\n- Use `.pose/knowledge/` only for time-bound operational memory: content\n  tied to a decision moment, a handoff between executions, or something\n  meant to be reviewed and eventually expire.\n- A durable fact or convention that will not become false and carries no\n  review trigger — a third-party tool's parsing quirk, a naming\n  convention, a recurring gotcha — is not a `decision-log` just because no\n  better type exists. Route it to `.pose/rules/` instead: add it to an\n  existing rule file or create one, then wire it into the relevant task\n  type's base `rules` array in `.pose/indexes/task-map.json` so\n  `pose suggest <task>` surfaces it automatically. No path scoping is\n  required — a task type's base `rules` list is returned regardless of\n  `--path`; only language/component-specific rules need the\n  `rules_by_domain` layer.\n- Rule of thumb: if nothing was decided or traded off, and nothing about\n  the fact will change on a predictable timeline, it is not a\n  `decision-log` and not an ADR — it is a rule. See ADR\n  `2026-08-15-durable-non-architectural-knowledge-belongs-in-rules-not-a-new-type`.\n\n## Reusable cross-execution format\n\n- Structure reusable context as a handoff with fixed sections: Context, Current state, Next checks, Risks, and Next owner.\n- Keep `source_refs` linked to the relevant spec, workflow, and executed check commands.\n- Record `last_reviewed_at` in the body to make effective cross-execution updates traceable.\n\n## Archiving and purging\n\n- Run biweekly triage to list expired artifacts.\n- Move expired artifacts to `.pose/knowledge/archive/` when they retain audit value.\n- Purge archived artifacts 180 days after expiration unless documented legal or compliance requirements apply.\n- Record every archive and purge action in the housekeeping log.\n\n## Sensitive content\n\n- Prohibit secrets, tokens, credentials, private keys, and equivalent material.\n- Prohibit personal data and non-anonymized customer data.\n- Do not copy restricted incidents or reports in full; keep only a controlled reference.\n- Classify frontmatter `sensitivity` as `public-internal` or `restricted`.\n- Remove identified sensitive content immediately and open a security follow-up.\n\n## Ownership and review\n\n- Keep `@pose-maintainers` as the primary governance owner.\n- Require an owner in every artifact frontmatter.\n- Review expiration biweekly and quality monthly.\n- Escalate a backlog overdue by more than two cycles to the primary owner.\n- Block backlog growth when `list-expired` exceeds the operational limit: zero in strict mode, two in tolerant mode.\n\n## Minimum operational checks\n\n- Run `pose knowledge-check --strict` biweekly to validate the expired backlog.\n- Run `pose knowledge-housekeeping list-expired` for detailed triage.\n- Run `pose knowledge-housekeeping archive-expired --dry-run` before applying changes.\n- Execute destructive actions only with an explicit `--apply` flag.\n.pose/knowledge/2026-08-14-decision-log-escalation-validate-native.md\n.pose/knowledge/2026-08-16-decision-log-module-metadata-discovery-invalidates-review-provenance.md\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"cat .pose/knowledge/2026-08-16-decision-log-module-metadata-discovery-invalidates-review-provenance.md; rg -n 'publishCanonicalValidationJSON|whenMatches|moduleValidationChecks' pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate*.go; sed -n '1,175p' pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_carry_test.go; rg -n -- '--carry|selfContained' POSE.md locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"cat .pose/knowledge/2026-08-16-decision-log-module-metadata-discovery-invalidates-review-provenance.md; rg -n 'publishCanonicalValidationJSON|whenMatches|moduleValidationChecks' pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate*.go; sed -n '1,175p' pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_carry_test.go; rg -n -- '--carry|selfContained' POSE.md locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\"","aggregated_output":"---\ntype: decision-log\nslug: module-metadata-discovery-invalidates-review-provenance\nowner: @pose-maintainers\nsensitivity: public-internal\ncreated_at: 2026-08-16\nlast_reviewed_at: 2026-08-16\nexpires_at: 2026-11-14\nsource_refs:\n  spec: \"pose-upgrade-path-audit-fixes\"\n  workflow: \"bugfix\"\n  commands: [\"pose update --force\", \"pose install\", \"pose index\", \"pose review bundle --seal\"]\n  external_sources: []\n---\n\n# decision-log: module-metadata-discovery-invalidates-review-provenance\n\n## Context\n\nAuditing `pose update`/`pose install` across 7 real repositories ahead of\nthe v1.4.0 release found that running either command on `pose-dist` itself\n(the product's own dogfooding instance) discovered\n`examples/brownfield-kits/direct-adoption/fixture/service/go.mod` — an\nillustrative fixture, not a real deliverable module — as a genuine module\nand added it to `.pose/indexes/module-metadata.json`. That single addition\nrecomputed the global provenance digest (`graph.ProvenanceDigest`, computed\nover the whole delivery-integrity graph, not per-spec — see the\n`pose-gate-closeout-procedure` session note this decision-log\ngeneralizes) and superseded the review attestation of every already-closed\nspec whose scope touches `module-metadata.json`: between 3 and 22 specs\nacross the audit's trial runs, depending on which other specs had most\nrecently closed.\n\n## Current state\n\nFixed the trigger only (spec `pose-upgrade-path-audit-fixes`, R7):\n`testdata`, `fixture` and `fixtures` directories are now excluded from\nmodule discovery in all three independent walkers\n(`discoverValidationModules` in validate.go, `scanModules` in index.go,\nand `internal/pose/discovery.go`'s capability scanner). A genuine,\nintentional module-metadata change — a real module added, removed or\nreclassified — still invalidates every closed spec's review attestation\nthat touches it, exactly as designed today. That broader behavior was\ndeliberately left unchanged (Decision 2 in the spec above): narrowing the\nprovenance digest so it only invalidates reviews whose scope actually\nincludes the changed module is a real design trade-off (what counts as\n\"unrelated,\" whether a narrower digest weakens the guarantee it exists to\nprovide) that deserves its own spec and explicit review, not a side effect\nof fixing a discovery false-positive.\n\n## Next checks\n\n- None deterministic yet. If a future spec pursues narrowing the\n  provenance digest, `pose-mcp/internal/pose/delivery_integrity.go`\n  (`ProvenanceDigest`) and `pose review bundle --seal`'s scope-provenance\n  matching are the two places to start reading.\n\n## Risks\n\n- Any *other* directory-discovery path added in the future (a fourth\n  scanner, or a stack detector that walks the tree independently) needs\n  the same `testdata`/`fixture`/`fixtures` exclusion applied by hand —\n  there is no single shared walker all three call today (each has its own\n  `ignored` map/condition; see the follow-up already on record about this\n  in `pose-review-bundle-roadmap-path-portability`/session knowledge about\n  \"enumerate by hand\" gaps recurring across this repository).\n- Every maintainer who runs `pose update --force`/`pose install` on\n  `pose-dist` itself after a *real* module-metadata change should expect\n  to reseal/reattest every closed spec whose review scope includes\n  `module-metadata.json`, per `pose-gate-closeout-procedure` — this is\n  expected, not a bug, and this decision-log's fix does not change it.\n\n## Next owner\n\n@pose-maintainers — no active handoff; revisit only if the digest-scoping\nfollow-up (spec `pose-upgrade-path-audit-fixes`, Follow-ups) is picked up.\n\n## References\n\n- spec:pose-upgrade-path-audit-fixes (Decision 2, R7, Non-goals)\n- knowledge: session note `pose-gate-closeout-procedure` (this repository's\n  own local agent memory, not a `.pose/knowledge/` artifact) — first\n  documented that the provenance digest is global-over-the-graph, not\n  per-spec, and that reseal/reattest is required after any reindex that\n  changes it.\npose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_carry.go:217:\t\tstack, checks, _ := moduleValidationChecks(module, matrix, \"\", \"\")\npose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_carry.go:399:\t\tstack, checks, _ := moduleValidationChecks(module, matrix, \"\", \"\")\npose-mcp/internal/cli/validate.go:1220:\t\t_, checks, selected := moduleValidationChecks(module, matrix, stackFilter, moduleFilter)\npose-mcp/internal/cli/validate.go:1512:\t\t\terr := publishCanonicalValidationJSONIn(store, writer.path, canonicalResultsPath, run)\npose-mcp/internal/cli/validate.go:1582:// moduleValidationChecks resolves the stack and checks one module runs after\npose-mcp/internal/cli/validate.go:1586:func moduleValidationChecks(module validationModule, matrix validationMatrix, stackFilter, moduleFilter string) (string, []validationCheck, bool) {\npose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_results.go:232:// publishCanonicalValidationJSON rechecks the delivery policy after acquiring\npose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_results.go:235:func publishCanonicalValidationJSON(root, relative, expectedPolicyPath string, run validationRunResult) error {\npose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_results.go:236:\treturn publishCanonicalValidationJSONIn(cliGovernedStore(root), relative, expectedPolicyPath, run)\npose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_results.go:239:func publishCanonicalValidationJSONIn(store posemodel.Store, relative, expectedPolicyPath string, run validationRunResult) error {\npackage cli\n\n// Carried checks (spec pose-validate-carries-unchanged-checks): validate\n// --carry refreshes the canonical result by running only what a change can\n// affect and carrying the rest with the head it really ran at.\n\nimport (\n\t\"crypto/sha256\"\n\t\"encoding/hex\"\n\t\"encoding/json\"\n\t\"os\"\n\t\"os/exec\"\n\t\"path/filepath\"\n\t\"sort\"\n\t\"strings\"\n\t\"testing\"\n\n\tposemodel \"github.com/harne8/pose-mcp/internal/pose\"\n)\n\nconst carryResults = \".pose/results/delivery-validation.json\"\n\ntype carryFixture struct {\n\tt       *testing.T\n\troot    string\n\tcounter string\n}\n\n// newCarryFixture builds a committed repository with Go modules a and b and\n// a root file. Every check appends its module to a counter file outside the\n// tree, so a test can tell an executed check from a carried one.\nfunc newCarryFixture(t *testing.T, metadata string) *carryFixture {\n\tt.Helper()\n\tf := &carryFixture{t: t, root: t.TempDir(), counter: filepath.Join(t.TempDir(), \"runs\")}\n\tf.write(\"a/go.mod\", \"module a\\n\")\n\tf.write(\"b/go.mod\", \"module b\\n\")\n\tf.write(\"root.txt\", \"root\\n\")\n\tf.write(\".gitignore\", \".pose/transfers/.authority-transfer.lock\\n\")\n\tf.write(\".pose/policy/delivery.json\", `{\"schema_version\":1,\"enabled\":true,\"results_path\":\"`+carryResults+`\"}`)\n\tmatrix := `{\"defaults\":{\"mode\":\"strict\"},\"stacks\":{\"go\":{\"checks\":[` +\n\t\t`{\"name\":\"ok\",\"program\":\"sh\",\"args\":[\"-c\",\"basename \\\"$PWD\\\" >> ` + f.counter + `; test ! -f fail\"],\"severity\":\"required\",\"evidenceClass\":\"unit\"}]}}}`\n\tf.write(\".pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json\", matrix)\n\tif metadata != \"\" {\n\t\tf.write(\".pose/indexes/module-metadata.json\", metadata)\n\t}\n\tf.git(\"init\", \"-q\")\n\tf.commit(\"base\")\n\treturn f\n}\n\nfunc (f *carryFixture) write(rel, content string) {\n\tf.t.Helper()\n\tpath := filepath.Join(f.root, rel)\n\tif err := os.MkdirAll(filepath.Dir(path), 0o755); err != nil {\n\t\tf.t.Fatal(err)\n\t}\n\tif err := os.WriteFile(path, []byte(content), 0o644); err != nil {\n\t\tf.t.Fatal(err)\n\t}\n}\n\nfunc (f *carryFixture) git(args ...string) string {\n\tf.t.Helper()\n\tcmd := exec.Command(\"git\", append([]string{\"-C\", f.root}, args...)...)\n\tcmd.Env = append(os.Environ(), \"GIT_AUTHOR_NAME=t\", \"GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL=t@t\", \"GIT_COMMITTER_NAME=t\", \"GIT_COMMITTER_EMAIL=t@t\", \"GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL=/dev/null\")\n\tout, err := cmd.CombinedOutput()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tf.t.Fatalf(\"git %v: %v %s\", args, err, out)\n\t}\n\treturn strings.TrimSpace(string(out))\n}\n\nfunc (f *carryFixture) commit(message string) string {\n\tf.t.Helper()\n\tf.git(\"add\", \"-A\")\n\tf.git(\"-c\", \"core.hooksPath=/dev/null\", \"commit\", \"-qm\", message, \"--allow-empty\")\n\treturn f.git(\"rev-parse\", \"HEAD\")\n}\n\n// validate runs pose validate and returns its exit code, output and the\n// canonical record it wrote. The counter is reset first.\nfunc (f *carryFixture) validate(args ...string) (int, string, validationRunResult) {\n\tf.t.Helper()\n\t_ = os.Remove(f.counter)\n\tcode, out := runValidate(f.t, f.root, append(args, \"--json-out\", carryResults)...)\n\tvar run validationRunResult\n\tif raw, err := os.ReadFile(filepath.Join(f.root, carryResults)); err == nil {\n\t\tif err := json.Unmarshal(raw, &run); err != nil {\n\t\t\tf.t.Fatal(err)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn code, out, run\n}\n\nfunc (f *carryFixture) ran() []string {\n\traw, err := os.ReadFile(f.counter)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn nil\n\t}\n\truns := strings.Fields(string(raw))\n\tsort.Strings(runs)\n\treturn runs\n}\n\nfunc checkOf(run validationRunResult, module string) checkResult {\n\tfor _, c := range run.Checks {\n\t\tif c.Module == module {\n\t\t\treturn c\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn checkResult{}\n}\n\n// baseline runs the full matrix, commits the canonical record and returns the\n// head the record validated.\nfunc (f *carryFixture) baseline() string {\n\tf.t.Helper()\n\tvalidated := f.git(\"rev-parse\", \"HEAD\")\n\tcode, out, _ := f.validate()\n\tif code != 0 {\n\t\tf.t.Fatalf(\"baseline exit=%d %s\", code, out)\n\t}\n\tf.commit(\"record\")\n\treturn validated\n}\n\nfunc TestCarryRunsOnlyTheChangedModule(t *testing.T) {\n\tf := newCarryFixture(t, \"\")\n\tbase := f.baseline()\n\tf.write(\"a/changed.go\", \"package a\\n\")\n\thead := f.commit(\"change a\")\n\tcode, out, run := f.validate(\"--carry\")\n\tif code != 0 {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"exit=%d %s\", code, out)\n\t}\n\tif got := strings.Join(f.ran(), \",\"); got != \"a\" {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"executed modules = %q, want only a\", got)\n\t}\n\tif run.Carry == nil || !run.Carry.Used || run.Carry.BaseHead != base {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"carry = %+v, want used from %s\", run.Carry, base)\n\t}\n\tif b := checkOf(run, \"b\"); b.Outcome != \"pass\" || b.CarriedFrom != base || b.CarriedGeneratedAt == \"\" {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"b = %+v, want a pass carried from %s\", b, base)\n\t}\n\tif a := checkOf(run, \"a\"); a.CarriedFrom != \"\" || a.Outcome != \"pass\" {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"a = %+v, want executed\", a)\n\t}\n\tif run.GitHead != head || run.Counts.Carried != 1 || run.Counts.Executed != 1 || run.Counts.Passed != 2 {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"head=%s counts=%+v\", run.GitHead, run.Counts)\n\t}\n\tif run.EngineVersion == \"\" {\n\t\tt.Fatal(\"engine_version must be recorded\")\n\t}\n}\n\nfunc TestCarryKeepsTheOriginalHeadAcrossChainedCarries(t *testing.T) {\n\tf := newCarryFixture(t, \"\")\n\tbase := f.baseline()\n\tf.write(\"a/one.go\", \"package a\\n\")\n\tf.commit(\"change a\")\n\tif code, out, _ := f.validate(\"--carry\"); code != 0 {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"exit=%d %s\", code, out)\n\t}\n\tf.commit(\"record again\")\n\tf.write(\"a/two.go\", \"package a\\n\")\n\tf.commit(\"change a again\")\n\t_, _, run := f.validate(\"--carry\")\n\tif b := checkOf(run, \"b\"); b.CarriedFrom != base {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"b carried from %q, want the head it ran at %s\", b.CarriedFrom, base)\n\t}\n}\n\nfunc TestCarryKeepsACarriedFailureAFailure(t *testing.T) {\n\tf := newCarryFixture(t, \"\")\n\tf.write(\"b/fail\", \"x\\n\")\nlocales/pt-BR/POSE.md:222:              [--changed-from rev [--changed-to rev] | --carry] [--jobs n] [--explain] [--emit-plan f]\nlocales/pt-BR/POSE.md:357:  **Escopo por mudança:** `--changed-from <rev> [--changed-to <rev>]` seleciona deterministicamente o conjunto mínimo seguro de módulos — módulos com arquivos alterados (rastreados e não rastreados), dependentes transitivos via arestas `dependsOn` em [`module-metadata.json`](.pose/indexes/module-metadata.json) e alargamento por política (criticidade `high` sempre roda). Os nomes alterados são lidos delimitados por NUL, e um rename nomeia a origem e o destino. Cada arquivo alterado seleciona o módulo dono mais específico, todo módulo que envolve esse dono (o módulo raiz envolve todos), todo módulo abaixo dele (um gitlink ou diretório substituído altera o que está abaixo) e todo módulo cujo `dependsOn` declarado o contém, está abaixo dele ou é a raiz `.`, mesmo depois que essa dependência é apagada. Um arquivo que nenhum módulo possui seleciona o módulo raiz. Metadados de módulo que não decodificam interrompem a seleção (execução completa com `--carry`, erro com `--changed-from`). Uma mudança fora de todos os módulos roda todo módulo que não declara `\"selfContained\": true` nos seus metadados (na incerteza, prefere-se execução segura; a declaração afirma que os checks do módulo só leem o próprio diretório e os módulos de `dependsOn`); checks não selecionados são registrados como skipped com o motivo da seleção e `--explain` imprime cada decisão. Revisões ficam confinadas a uma gramática segura; sem as flags, a validação completa é inalterada.\nlocales/pt-BR/POSE.md:359:  **Checks carregados:** `--carry --json-out <results_path>` renova o resultado canônico em vez de rodar a matriz inteira de novo. Ele usa o resultado canônico atual como base quando a base é comparável: mesmo schema, modo, digest da matriz e `engine_version` (a versão do motor mais a revisão VCS da qual o binário foi compilado; um build de árvore modificada nunca carrega), sem filtro, não cancelada, com `material_clean` registrado (validou uma cópia de trabalho inalterada do próprio head: nenhum material rastreado ou não rastreado e não ignorado mudou, e o HEAD não se moveu durante o run, segundo o reflog; mudanças de modo contam qualquer que seja `core.fileMode`, e todo submódulo precisa estar no commit registrado, verificado antes da leitura do plano e no fim do run) e sem binding de material indisponível, com `git_head` ancestral de `HEAD` cópia de trabalho limpa agora nos paths materiais sistema de arquivos que diferencia maiúsculas, sem diretório com casefold no caminho até um módulo ou uma dependência, nenhum módulo alcançado por symlink ou compartilhando diretório com outro módulo, nenhum symlink rastreado cujo path ou alvo esteja num módulo que não seja a raiz ou num `dependsOn` declarado (ou cujo alvo saia do repositório), inclusive na árvore de todo submódulo (um submódulo sem checkout ou com submódulos aninhados recusa), nenhuma dependência declarada fora do repositório ou escrita como path não limpo ou que resolve por symlink (rastreado ou ignorado), apenas paths ASCII de módulo e dependência, nenhuma entrada de índice marcada assume-unchanged ou skip-worktree, e nenhum id de check definido duas vezes. Os nomes alterados são lidos com todo gitlink visível, qualquer que seja a configuração do Git. O head é fixado antes do planejamento, e um run cujo HEAD se moveu registra seus bindings de material como indisponíveis. Roda os módulos que as regras de escopo por mudança selecionam entre o head da base e `HEAD`; o arquivo do resultado canônico e os registros administrativos derivados que o binding de material ignora (arquivos de dados ainda regulares nos diretórios de registros derivados sob `.pose/`: índices, resultados, estado, assessments e registros de revisão; a matriz de validação continua material) não contam como mudança. Um módulo cujos checks não estão todos na base também roda. Todo outro check mantém o resultado da base, inclusive o desfecho, e registra `carried_from` (o head em que rodou, mantido em carregamentos encadeados) e `carried_generated_at`; o índice de entrega lê esse head no resultado, então o frescor da evidência julga o commit que um check carregado realmente observou. Uma falha carregada continua reprovando o run. Qualquer condição não atendida roda tudo, e `carry.reason` diz por quê. `carry` registra a base, o número de arquivos alterados e o motivo de cada módulo selecionado, e `counts.carried` os checks reaproveitados. `--carry` não combina com `--module`, `--stack`, `--workspace`, `--root-only`, `--changed-from` ou `--changed-to`.\nPOSE.md:218:              [--changed-from rev [--changed-to rev] | --carry] [--jobs n] [--explain] [--emit-plan f]\nPOSE.md:353:  **Changed scope:** `--changed-from <rev> [--changed-to <rev>]` selects the minimum safe module set deterministically — modules containing changed files (tracked and untracked), transitive dependents via `dependsOn` edges in [`module-metadata.json`](.pose/indexes/module-metadata.json), and policy widening (criticality `high` always runs). Changed names are read NUL-delimited and a rename names both its source and its destination. Each changed file selects its most specific owning module, every module enclosing that owner (the root module encloses them all), every module beneath it (a gitlink or replaced directory changes what lies under it), and every module whose declared `dependsOn` contains it, lies beneath it or is the root `.`, even after that dependency is deleted. A file no module owns selects the root module. Module metadata that does not decode stops the selection (a full run under `--carry`, an error under `--changed-from`). A change outside every module runs every module that does not declare `\"selfContained\": true` in its metadata (uncertainty prefers safe execution; the declaration states that the module's checks read only its own directory and its `dependsOn` modules); unselected checks are recorded as skipped with the selection reason and `--explain` prints every decision. Revisions are confined to a safe grammar; without the flags, full validation is unchanged.\nPOSE.md:355:  **Carried checks:** `--carry --json-out <results_path>` refreshes the canonical result instead of rerunning the whole matrix. It uses the current canonical result as its base when the base is comparable: same schema, mode, matrix digest and `engine_version` (the engine version plus the VCS revision the binary was built from; a build of a modified tree never carries), unfiltered, not cancelled, recorded `material_clean` (it validated an unchanged working copy of its own head: no tracked or untracked unignored material changed and HEAD did not move while it ran, as its reflog shows; mode changes count whatever `core.fileMode` says, and every submodule must hold its recorded commit, checked both before the plan is read and when the run ends) with no unavailable material binding, with its `git_head` an ancestor of `HEAD`, a clean material working copy now, a case-sensitive filesystem with no case-folded directory on the path to a module or a dependency, no module reached through a symlink or sharing its directory with another module, no tracked symlink whose path or target lies in a module other than the root or in a declared `dependsOn` (or whose target leaves the repository), every submodule's tree included (a submodule that is not checked out or has nested submodules refuses), no declared dependency outside the repository or written as an unclean path or resolving through a symlink (tracked or ignored), only ASCII module and dependency paths, no index entry marked assume-unchanged or skip-worktree, and no check id defined twice. Changed names are read with every gitlink shown, whatever the Git configuration. The head is pinned before planning, and a run whose HEAD moved records its material bindings as unavailable. It runs the modules the changed-scope rules select between the base head and `HEAD`; the canonical result file and the derived administrative records that material bindings ignore (data files that are still regular files in the derived-record directories under `.pose/`: indexes, results, state, assessments and review records; the validation matrix stays material) are not changes. A module whose checks are not all in the base also runs. Every other check keeps the base result, its outcome included, and records `carried_from` (the head it ran at, kept through chained carries) and `carried_generated_at`; the delivery index reads that head for the result, so evidence freshness judges the commit a carried check really observed. A carried failure still fails the run. Any unmet condition runs everything, and `carry.reason` says why. `carry` records the base, the number of changed files and each selected module's reason, and `counts.carried` the reused checks. `--carry` does not combine with `--module`, `--stack`, `--workspace`, `--root-only`, `--changed-from` or `--changed-to`.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
